Create readable memory facts for the supplied subject from canonical assertions. Preserve every assertion's
substantive meaning and assign each supplied claim alias to exactly one fact.

For each fact, choose memory_scope first: identify the specific occurrence, particular object property, relationship,
or ongoing state this fact explains. Identify the shared referent before its attributes. For assertions about one specific event, use that event as
the scope and combine its complementary attributes, even when their predicates differ.
For a plan or habit, identify its particular action and object as the scope. For a preference, identify the
specific preference being stated. For an event, identify one occurrence. A fact represents one such memory
with its supporting details. Repeated support for one assertion belongs together. Complementary details of one occurrence belong together.
Different occurrences and independently useful commitments receive separate facts, even when they share a person,
interest, conversation, or section. Use as many facts as the distinct memories require.

Choose members before writing text. Write one clear sentence for their shared memory, retaining names, timing,
location, quantities, direction, reasons, attribution, uncertainty, and commitment level. Each clause needs support
from that fact's members. For a singleton, return text=null; the application copies its canonical text exactly. For equivalent assertions, express the
meaning once. Preserve substantive detail by separating memories into additional facts when needed.

Read existing display groups as previous presentation choices. Re-evaluate their member boundaries using the
canonical assertions. Reviewed corrections in canonical text take precedence over older display wording.
Keep the two sides of review-required changes in separate ordinary groups for the review workflow.

Choose an allowed section. Set state="history" for completed occurrences and ended states, and "current" for
ongoing states, future plans, recurring habits, and timeless knowledge. Source timestamps anchor relative words;
use an explicit temporal record when available. Preserve temporal limits in the sentence.

Return facts with concrete memory_scope, member_claim_aliases, state, section_key, text, confidence, and reason.
